Build a crystal model from translations and an atomic basis (@mp-lattice: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mineralogy-and-petrology, #source "../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP", #source "../sources/Crystallography-Open-Database.lingua#r_0GWYXPDY9KYA4V4AXPCEFJG9RG", #source "../sources/IUCr-Crystallographic-Teaching.lingua#r_334MQS5KEVRJ17XKK0WKCP6KWX", #mineralogy-and-petrology "Cell-metric-and-reciprocal-geometry.lingua#r_6CB07MYV3Z3DK5KBGKFET4K8SM", #materials-engineering "../../../../hephaestus/engineering/materials-engineering/structure/Packing-neighbours-and-density-in-simple-crystals.lingua#r_43380X5EW4XK713V0F66KH2RV4") { r_2HS07Q4PD6XHTVGDHBJ3SDB6J3
**Read:** [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the complete unit-cell, lattice, and crystal-structure sections; [Crystallography Open Database — structures, citations, and CIF records](../sources/Crystallography-Open-Database.lingua#r_0GWYXPDY9KYA4V4AXPCEFJG9RG), one identified structure record and its original paper; [Crystallographic teaching pamphlets — IUCr](../sources/IUCr-Crystallographic-Teaching.lingua#r_334MQS5KEVRJ17XKK0WKCP6KWX), the full relevant lattice/symmetry teaching treatment.

A lattice describes translational repetition; a basis describes the contents attached to that repetition. A crystal structure combines them. A lattice point need not be an atom, and changing the chosen unit cell need not change the physical structure. Distinguish a primitive cell from a useful conventional cell.

Coordinates within a cell are fractional relative to its basis vectors. Translate them into Cartesian coordinates before taking ordinary distances or angles. A coordinate difference across a cell boundary may need periodic translations to identify the relevant neighbor, while an unqualified shortest distance is not automatically a chemically bonded pair.

For cell volume V, Z formula units, molar mass M, and Avogadro constant N_A, an ideal crystallographic density is ρ = ZM/(N_A V). An invented orthogonal cell with edges 0.4, 0.5, and 0.6 nm, Z = 4, and M = 100 g mol⁻¹ gives about 5.535 g cm⁻³. The result assumes those contents and dimensions, not a measured density of a named mineral.

**Work:** construct a simple cell with an explicit basis, count shared boundary contents correctly, and derive the density example with independent unit checks. Compare two cell choices for one authorized structure record. Identify how vacancies, mixed occupancy, composition, or measurement conditions would affect the density comparison.

Read the complete lattice/unit-cell treatment and the original structural reference for the selected record. The next step develops the metric needed for nonorthogonal cells and diffraction geometry.

**Continue:** [Calculate geometry with the cell metric and reciprocal basis](Cell-metric-and-reciprocal-geometry.lingua#r_6CB07MYV3Z3DK5KBGKFET4K8SM).

